  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nAnother round of fairly lackluster weather continues today. A \ncumulus field to the north of the International Border is producing \nsome scattered rain showers in Ontario, which does have a low \n(<10%) chance to bleed over the border this afternoon and \nevening. This would lead to 0.01\" of rain or less if it does \noccur. A heavier band of wildfire smoke aloft is preventing more\ncumulus fields from developing across a good portion of the \nNorthland today. \n\nOvernight, a slug of moisture from the Dakotas is expected to move \nover central MN and WI, which could prompt some areas of scattered \nrain showers in the Northland, with the highest chances along the \nsouthern border of the CWA from the Brainerd Lakes area into NW WI. \nNo severe weather is expected and even producing any thunderstorms \nmight be tough. There is a lot of dry air preceding any of this \nprecipitation and it will be losing support as it moves into the \nNorthland, so it is likely that this activity would be scattered in \nnature with light rainfall amounts. As that area of moisture and \nassociated weak shortwave push east through the day Thursday, it \nwill likely continue to lead to areas of clouds and could even \nprompt a couple afternoon showers but confidence is very low on this \noccurring. No severe weather nor heavy rainfall is expected. \n\nLate week into the weekend, the upper level ridge to our south \nshifts east, with a shortwave disturbance running along the top of \nit. This is expected to bring an area of showers and thunderstorms \nto the Northland. Global ensembles show that overall this will \nprobably be a mostly light rain for most - up to a couple tenths of \nan inch. However, if some thunderstorms are able to embed, a few \nlucky folks could see locally higher amounts around an inch. At this \npoint, not expecting any severe weather at this time. There is some \nlingering uncertainty regarding the timing of this precipitation, \nwhich largely has to do with the timing of the broader ridge \nshifting east. Some models bring it in as early as Friday evening \nwhile others a much more delayed more into Saturday afternoon. \n\nAs we remain on the north side of the ridge through the 7 day \nperiod, expect temperatures to stay largely right around normal with \nhigh temperatures in the 70s to mid 80s and overnight lows in the \n50s and 60s.",
